[tool_result]1	--- 2	name: parametric-3d-printing 3	description: "Use this skill when the user wants to design a 3D-printable physical object they intend to manufacture. Triggers: any mention of '3D print', 'STL', 'parametric model', 'Gridfinity', 'enclosure', 'bracket', 'mount', 'case', 'housing', 'CadQuery', 'OpenSCAD', or a specific FDM printer (Bambu Lab, Prusa, Ender); questions about print-friendly design, snap-fits, tolerances, or wall thickness; and requests for functional parts like Arduino enclosures, cable organizers, wall mounts, adapters, or mechanical components. Also fires when the user describes a real physical object to make, provided the goal is to manufacture it. Do NOT use for: 3D rendering, animation, game assets, digital-only art, photogrammetry, sculpting, editing an existing STL file the user already has, or any 3D work that is not heading toward a printer." 4	--- 5	 6	# Parametric 3D Printing with CadQuery 7	 8	## Overview 9	 10	This skill generates parametric 3D models using **CadQuery** (Python) and exports them as STL files ready for slicing. CadQuery is preferred because it installs via pip, has a Pythonic API, and handles complex geometry (fillets, chamfers, booleans, assemblies) better than alternatives. 11	 12	## Setup 13	 14	```bash 15	# CadQuery requires Python 3.10-3.12 (OCC kernel lacks 3.13+ wheels) 16	python3.12 -m venv .venv && source .venv/bin/activate 17	 18	# Install CadQuery and preview dependencies 19	pip install cadquery trimesh pyrender Pillow numpy lxml 20	``` 21	 22	CadQuery uses the OpenCASCADE kernel under the hood. trimesh, pyrender, Pillow, and numpy are used for the preview-analyze-iterate loop; lxml is required by the 3MF writer (`stl_to_3mf.py`). No display server is needed; everything renders headlessly via pyrender's offscreen backend. 23	 24	**If CadQuery fails to install** (OCC kernel build errors), try: 25	```bash 26	# Option 1: Use conda (CadQuery's officially recommended method) 27	conda install -c cadquery -c conda-forge cadquery 28	 29	# Option 2: Use the pre-built wheels 30	pip install cadquery --find-links https://github.com/CadQuery/CadQuery/releases 31	``` 32	 33	## Real-World Dimension Research 34	 35	When designing objects that interface with real products (phones, chargers, PCBs, connectors, etc.), **use web search to find accurate dimensions** before writing any geometry code. Don't guess or use approximate values. Even 1-2mm off can make a part unusable. 36	 37	**What to research:** 38	- Connector/port dimensions (USB-C: 8.4 x 2.6mm opening, Lightning, barrel jacks) 39	- Device dimensions (phone width/thickness, PCB footprints, charger puck diameters) 40	- Mounting hole patterns and screw sizes (M2.5, M3, etc.) 41	- Standard component specs (MagSafe puck: 56mm diameter, 5.6mm thick) 42	- Cable bend radii and strain relief requirements 43	 44	**How to use it:** 45	1. Search for "[product] dimensions mm" or "[component] datasheet" 46	2. Cross-reference at least 2 sources when precision matters 47	3. Add the sourced dimensions as comments in the PARAMETERS section: 48	   ```python 49	   # MagSafe puck dimensions (source: Apple spec + iFixit teardown) 50	   puck_diameter = 56.0    # mm 51	   puck_thickness = 5.6    # mm 52	   ``` 53	4. When in doubt, add 0.3-0.5mm clearance to external dimensions 54	 55	This is especially important for: phone cases/stands, charger mounts, PCB enclosures, cable management, adapter fittings, and anything that clips onto or wraps around an existing product. 56	 57	## Core Workflow 58	 59	1. **Gather requirements** (see Requirements Gathering below) 60	2. **Research dimensions** of any real-world products involved (see above) 61	3. **Phase 1, Base shape**: Build outer shell, preview, get user feedback 62	4. **Phase 2, Features**: Add functional details, preview, get user feedback 63	5. **Phase 3, Final delivery**: Fillets, cleanup, final preview + STL + print recommendations 64	6. **Offer parameter tweaks** after delivery 65	 66	This is a **collaborative, show-as-you-go** process. Do NOT disappear and come back with a finished model. Show the user your progress at each phase and incorporate their feedback before moving on. 67	 68	## Requirements Gathering 69	 70	Before writing any code, walk through these topics with the user **conversationally**. Don't dump all questions at once. Ask the most important ones first, then follow up based on answers. Use reasonable defaults when the user doesn't specify. 71	 72	**What is it?** 73	Object type, purpose, what it holds/protects/attaches to. Get a clear mental model of the object before anything else. 74	 75	**Critical dimensions** 76	Must-fit measurements, like PCB size, phone width, screw spacing, diameter of the thing it wraps around, etc. These are non-negotiable and drive everything else. 77	 78	**Mounting & attachment** 79	How does it connect to things? Screws (what size?), snap-fit, adhesive tape, magnets, freestanding on a desk? This affects wall thickness, boss placement, and overall structure. 80	 81	**Printer & material** 82	What printer do they have? (Bambu, Prusa, Ender, etc.) Nozzle size? Material (PLA, PETG, TPU)? This directly affects tolerances, minimum feature sizes, and design constraints. Defaults: 0.4mm nozzle, PLA, 0.2mm layer height. 83	 84	**Functional needs** 85	Ventilation/airflow, water resistance, cable routing, access panels, visibility windows, stacking, weight limits. Ask only what's relevant to the object. 86	 87	**Aesthetic preferences** 88	Rounded vs sharp edges, minimal vs industrial look, color considerations (affects visibility of layer lines). Ask briefly. Most users care more about function than form. 89	 90	Start with the first two (what + dimensions), then ask about mounting and material if relevant. Only ask about aesthetics if the user seems to care or if it affects structural choices. 91	 92	## Progressive Preview Workflow 93	 94	Build the model in phases. At each phase, export an STL, render a preview, self-review it, then **show it to the user and ask for feedback** before proceeding. This catches problems early and keeps the user involved. 95	 96	### Preview recipe (use at every phase) 97	 98	**One-shot (run script + render + parse result as JSON):** 99	```bash 100	python3 run_cadquery_model.py model.py --preview --strict 101	``` 102	This executes `model.py`, finds the STL it wrote, renders the multi-view preview, and emits a JSON result with `success`, `stdout`, `stderr`, `stl`, `preview`, and `watertight`. With `--strict`, a non-watertight mesh is a hard failure. Use this as the default loop: if `success` is false, read the `stderr` field to fix the CadQuery script, then re-run. 103	 104	**Rendering only (when the STL already exists):** 105	```bash 106	python3 preview.py model.stl preview.png --views multi 107	``` 108	 109	Then view the preview image, self-review it against the checklist in `design-review.md`, and fix any issues you spot **before** showing it to the user. 110	 111	--- 112	 113	### Phase 1: Base Shape 114	 115	Build the basic outer form: overall dimensions, shell/walls, bottom plate. No cutouts, no fillets, no details yet. 116	 117	1. Write the script with parameters and basic geometry 118	2. Export STL and render preview 119	3. Self-review: Does the shape and size look right? Is the bottom flat for printing? 120	4. **Show the preview to the user**: "Here's the basic shape. Does this look right before I add details?" Include key dimensions. 121	5. Wait for feedback. If the user wants changes, iterate here before moving on. 122	 123	### Phase 2: Features 124	 125	Add functional details: holes, cutouts, mounting bosses, cable slots, ventilation, snap-fits, internal structures. 126	 127	1. Add features to the script 128	2. Export STL and render preview 129	3. Self-review: Are all features visible? Do booleans look clean? Are holes in the right positions? 130	4. **Show the preview to the user**: "I've added [list features]. Anything to change before I finalize?" 131	5. Wait for feedback. Iterate if needed. 132	 133	### Phase 3: Final Delivery 134	 135	Apply finishing touches: fillets, chamfers, edge cleanup. Do a full printability review. 136	 137	1. Add fillets/chamfers (largest radius first, apply after shell) 138	2. Export final STL and render preview 139	3. **Full self-review** using the complete checklist from `design-review.md`: visual inspection, dimensional verification, printability analysis 140	4. Fix any issues found, re-export if needed 141	5. **Deliver to the user**: final STL + preview image + print recommendations (orientation, supports, infill, material notes) 142	 143	--- 144	 145	**Important:** Do NOT skip phases or combine them unless the model is very simple (e.g., a flat bracket with two holes). For anything with enclosed geometry, multiple features, or tight tolerances, follow all three phases. 146	 147	Read `design-review.md` for the full visual inspection checklist, dimensional verification code, and printability analysis helpers. 148	 149	### Print Recommendations (final delivery) 150	 151	When you deliver the final STL, always include a one-line slicer recipe plus a short rationale. Bambu Studio, PrusaSlicer, and OrcaSlicer already set sensible defaults from their filament + process presets, so **do not restate every slicer option**. Only tell the user what matters for *this* model: material, layer height, walls, infill, supports, and orientation. Tweak from the baseline below only when the model needs it. 152	 153	**Baseline recipe (0.4mm nozzle, typical FDM):** 154	> PLA, 0.2mm layer, 2 walls, 15% gyroid infill, no supports, orientation: flat side on bed. 155	 156	**When to deviate from the baseline:** 157	- **Load-bearing brackets / hooks / hinges**: bump infill to 25-40%, 3-4 walls, consider PETG over PLA for toughness. 158	- **Thin decorative walls or vases**: 0 infill, vase mode or 1 wall. 159	- **Tall narrow parts**: add a brim for bed adhesion. 160	- **Flexible parts (gaskets, grips)**: TPU 95A, 0.2mm layer, slower speed, no supports. 161	- **Functional overhangs the geometry can't avoid**: tree supports, or call them out so the user knows. 162	- **Outdoor / hot environments**: PETG or ASA, not PLA. 163	- **Food / skin contact**: call out that FDM parts are not food-safe and recommend a food-safe coating. 164	 165	**Format at delivery time:** 166	``` 167	Print settings: PLA, 0.2mm layer, 2 walls, 15% gyroid infill, no supports. 168	Orientation: place flat back side on the bed (front face up). 169	Why: the case has no overhangs above 45°, and 15% infill is plenty for a 170	TPU-adjacent protective shell. 171	``` 172	 173	Keep it to ~3 lines. Never dump every slicer setting; the slicer already knows. 174	 175	## Script Template 176	 177	ALWAYS structure scripts like this: 178	 179	```python 180	import cadquery as cq 181	 182	# ============================================================ 183	# PARAMETERS - Edit these to customize the model 184	# ============================================================ 185	# Overall dimensions 186	width = 60.0        # mm - outer width 187	depth = 40.0        # mm - outer depth   188	height = 25.0       # mm - outer height 189	 190	# Wall and structural 191	wall = 2.0          # mm - wall thickness (min 1.2 for FDM) 192	corner_r = 2.0      # mm - corner fillet radius 193	 194	# Tolerances 195	fit_clearance = 0.3 # mm - clearance for press-fit (adjust per printer) 196	 197	# ============================================================ 198	# MODEL 199	# ============================================================ 200	result = ( 201	    cq.Workplane("XY") 202	    .box(width, depth, height, centered=(True, True, False)) 203	    # ... build geometry using parameters above (bottom at Z=0) 204	) 205	 206	# ============================================================ 207	# EXPORT 208	# ============================================================ 209	# Use tolerance=0.01, angularTolerance=0.1 for consistent tessellation 210	# across models. Defaults give coarser, wildly variable STL sizes. 211	cq.exporters.export(result, "output.stl", 212	                    tolerance=0.01, angularTolerance=0.1) 213	print(f"Exported: {width}x{depth}x{height}mm") 214	``` 215	 216	## Key Rules 217	 218	### Parameters First 219	- ALL dimensions go in the PARAMETERS section at the top 220	- Use descriptive names: `screw_hole_d`, not `d1` 221	- Add units in comments (always mm) 222	- Group related parameters with blank lines and section comments 223	 224	### Print-Friendly Defaults 225	Key FDM design defaults: 226	 227	| Property | Minimum | Recommended | 228	|----------|---------|-------------| 229	| Wall thickness | 1.2mm | 2.0mm | 230	| Layer height | 0.08mm | 0.2mm | 231	| Hole clearance | 0.2mm | 0.3mm | 232	| Press-fit interference | 0.1mm | 0.15mm | 233	| Min feature size | 0.4mm (nozzle) | 0.8mm | 234	| Fillet radius (bottom) | 0.5mm | 1.0mm | 235	| Bridge span | - | < 20mm unsupported | 236	| Overhang angle | - | < 45 degrees from vertical | 237	 238	**Material-specific adjustments:** TPU needs larger clearances (~0.5mm) due to flex. PETG is stickier, so add +0.1mm to fit clearances. ABS shrinks ~0.5-0.7%, so scale critical dimensions up slightly. When in doubt, print a small test piece first. 239	 240	### Orientation Awareness 241	- Design with print orientation in mind 242	- Flat bottom surfaces print best 243	- Avoid supports when possible by designing around overhangs 244	- Add chamfers to bottom edges instead of fillets (fillets need supports) 245	- Comment the intended print orientation in the script 246	 247	### CadQuery Patterns 248	 249	Common patterns to know: 250	 251	**Hollow enclosure (boolean subtraction, preferred):** 252	```python 253	outer = ( 254	    cq.Workplane("XY") 255	    .box(width, depth, height, centered=(True, True, False)) 256	    .edges("|Z").fillet(corner_r) 257	) 258	inner = ( 259	    cq.Workplane("XY") 260	    .workplane(offset=floor_t) 261	    .box(width - 2*wall, depth - 2*wall, height, centered=(True, True, False)) 262	    .edges("|Z").fillet(max(0.1, corner_r - wall)) 263	) 264	result = outer.cut(inner) 265	``` 266	 267	**Screw boss:** 268	```python 269	.pushPoints([(x, y)]) 270	.circle(boss_od / 2).extrude(boss_h) 271	.pushPoints([(x, y)]) 272	.hole(screw_d + fit_clearance) 273	``` 274	 275	**Snap-fit clip:** 276	```python 277	# Cantilever beam with overhang hook 278	.workplane(offset=wall) 279	.moveTo(x, y).rect(clip_w, clip_l).extrude(clip_h) 280	# Add hook at tip with a small overhang (< 45 deg) 281	``` 282	 283	**Ventilation grid:** 284	```python 285	.pushPoints(vent_positions) 286	.slot2D(slot_l, slot_w).cutThruAll() 287	``` 288	 289	Other patterns: mounting brackets, cable routing channels, text/labels (`.text()`), multi-part assemblies with alignment pins. 290	 291	### Common Pitfalls 292	 293	- **Hollowing: prefer boolean subtraction over `.shell()`**. `.shell()` is fragile. It fails on tapered bodies, lofted shapes, unions of multiple primitives, and anything with many fillets. The reliable pattern is: 294	  ```python 295	  outer = cq.Workplane("XY").box(w, d, h, centered=(True, True, False)).edges("|Z").fillet(corner_r) 296	  inner = ( 297	      cq.Workplane("XY") 298	      .workplane(offset=floor_t) 299	      .box(w - 2*wall, d - 2*wall, h, centered=(True, True, False)) 300	      .edges("|Z").fillet(max(0.1, corner_r - wall)) 301	  ) 302	  result = outer.cut(inner) 303	  ``` 304	  Only reach for `.shell()` when the body is a single simple primitive (one `.box()` or `.cylinder()`) with a uniform wall thickness on all sides. If in doubt, use boolean subtraction. 305	- **Build order: fillet → cut, not cut → fillet**. Apply fillets while the geometry is still a clean primitive. Once you have cut holes/slots/pockets into a body, filleting the resulting edges often fails or produces bad geometry. Same rule for chamfers. 306	- **Fillet failures**: Apply fillets from largest to smallest radius. **Do not wrap fillets in `try/except` to silently shrink the radius.** A fillet failure means the geometry or the radius is wrong; find the root cause (too-large radius, wall thinner than radius, adjacent faces that the fillet would degenerate) and fix that. 307	- **Zero-thickness geometry**: Ensure boolean operations don't create infinitely thin walls. Add a small epsilon (0.01mm) when cutting bodies that are meant to pass just through a surface. 308	- **Coordinate system**: CadQuery centers geometry at origin by default. Use `centered=(True, True, False)` on `.box()` to place the bottom at Z=0 so `.faces("<Z")` is always the print bed. 309	- **Hole direction**: `.hole()` cuts through the entire part by default. Use `.cboreHole()` or `.cskHole()` for counterbore/countersink. 310	- **Taper direction**: In `.extrude(taper=angle)`, a **positive** taper angle narrows the shape (draft inward), **negative** flares it outward. This is opposite to what many people expect. 311	- **Loft is fragile**: `.loft()` fails on many cross-section combinations. Prefer `.extrude(taper=angle)` when transitioning between a shape and a scaled version of itself. Only use `.loft()` when you need to transition between genuinely different profiles (e.g., circle to rectangle). 312	- **Export errors / non-watertight STL**: If export fails or the preview reports a non-watertight mesh, the geometry is invalid (usually self-intersecting booleans or zero-thickness faces). Fix the cause, don't paper over it. Run `python3 preview.py model.stl --strict` to fail loudly on non-watertight output. 313	 314	## Export 315	 316	```python 317	# STL (for slicing) - always set tolerance + angularTolerance for 318	# consistent tessellation. Defaults produce variable file sizes and 319	# over-tessellated glyphs on text features. 320	cq.exporters.export(result, "model.stl", 321	                    tolerance=0.01, angularTolerance=0.1) 322	 323	# STEP (for further CAD editing) 324	cq.exporters.export(result, "model.step") 325	``` 326	 327	Always export STL for printing. Optionally export STEP if the user might want to edit in Fusion 360 or similar. 328	 329	## Multi-Part Models 330	 331	For models with multiple parts (e.g., enclosure + lid): 332	 333	```python 334	# Export each part separately 335	cq.exporters.export(body, "enclosure_body.stl") 336	cq.exporters.export(lid, "enclosure_lid.stl") 337	``` 338	 339	Name files descriptively so the user knows which part is which. 340	 341	## Gridfinity Bins 342	 343	For any Gridfinity request (bins, inserts, cradles for the 42mm modular grid system), **use the bundled `gridfinity.py` module; never hand-roll the base profile**. The module is tested, spec-compatible (base profile, stacking lip, 6x2mm magnet bores, M3 screw holes on the 26mm square), and validates parameters with actionable errors. 344	 345	**Workflow:** 346	1. Copy `gridfinity.py` from this skill's directory next to the model script (it is a vendored module; generated scripts must stay runnable standalone). 347	2. Build with the `GridfinityBin` API: 348	 349	```python 350	import cadquery as cq 351	from gridfinity import GridfinityBin 352	 353	bin = GridfinityBin(grid_x=3, grid_y=2, height_units=3, 354	                    stacking_lip=True, magnets=False) 355	bin.add_pocket(length=88.6, width=72.6, corner_r=(12.0, 1.0))  # cradle 356	# or: bin.add_compartments(cols=3, rows=2, scoop_r=6, label_tab=True) 357	# or: bin.add_polygon_pocket(points, depth=15, clearance=0.3) 358	# or: bin.add_cylinder_pocket(diameter=22, center=(42, 27),  # finger well; overlapping pockets merge 359	#         tilt=35, tilt_dir=(-1, 0), round_bottom=True)      # smooth angled thumb scoop 360	# or: bin.add_finger_notch(side="+Y", width=20, offset=40)   # offset slides it along the wall 361	result = bin.build() 362	cq.exporters.export(result, "my_bin.stl", tolerance=0.01, angularTolerance=0.1) 363	print(bin.summary()) 364	``` 365	 366	**Cheat sheet:** 367	- Spec constants: grid pitch 42mm, height unit 7mm. Every derived number (footprint, lip height, floor Z, usable cavity depth) is exposed on the builder: `bin.outer_w`, `bin.outer_d`, `bin.total_h`, `bin.floor_z`, `bin.max_depth`, and `bin.summary()` prints them all. Read those instead of recomputing. 368	- Sizing an object pocket: add 0.3mm per side fit clearance, plus ~1mm per side if the object must drop in and out easily. 369	- Pocket matching a real object's shape: `outline_from_scan.py` extracts the max cross-section outline from an STL scan of the object (align, scale to spec dims, slice, union), ready for `add_polygon_pocket`. 370	- Height choice: whatever holds the contents; the contents may protrude above the rim (see `examples/gridfinity_d110_bin.py`). 371	- `magnets=True` needs a magnetic baseplate; skip it for drawer bins. 372	 373	The builder raises `GridfinityError` with a specific message when a pocket is too big, a wall too thin, or the floor too shallow for screws (a few checks fire in the `add_*` calls, the rest in `build()`). Fix the parameters; do not bypass the validation. 374	 375	If the user asks for something the module does not cover (baseplates, odd lips), fall back to custom geometry but keep the spec constants from `gridfinity.py` as the source of truth. When delivering a release, ship `gridfinity.py` alongside the model script. 376	 377	## Parameter Adjustment Offer 378	 379	After delivering the final model, **always present the key parameters as a summary table** and offer to tweak them. This lets the user fine-tune without re-explaining the whole design. 380	 381	Example: 382	``` 383	Here's your final model! Current parameters: 384	 385	| Parameter       | Value  | 386	|----------------|--------| 387	| Width          | 90 mm  | 388	| Depth          | 65 mm  | 389	| Height         | 30 mm  | 390	| Wall thickness | 4 mm   | 391	| Cable slot     | 18 mm  | 392	| Corner radius  | 3 mm   | 393	| Fit clearance  | 0.3 mm | 394	 395	Want me to adjust anything? Just say e.g. "make it 5mm taller" or "wider cable slot." 396	``` 397	 398	Only include parameters the user would plausibly want to change. Skip internal constants like `eps` or `nozzle_d`. Group them logically (dimensions first, then structural, then tolerances). 399	 400	## Output Checklist 401	 402	Before delivering a model, verify: 403	- [ ] All dimensions are parameterized (no magic numbers in geometry code) 404	- [ ] Wall thickness >= 1.2mm 405	- [ ] Designed for printability (minimal overhangs/supports) 406	- [ ] Print orientation noted in comments 407	- [ ] STL exported and file size is reasonable (not 0 bytes) 408	- [ ] Clear parameter names with units 409	- [ ] Script runs without errors 410	- [ ] **Multi-view preview generated and visually inspected** 411	- [ ] **Preview shows correct shape, features, and proportions** 412	- [ ] **Bounding box dimensions match requirements** 413	- [ ] Both STL and preview PNG delivered to user 414
